Article 23 The contents of an energy audit shall include:
(1) reviewing the building completion acceptance documents and the ledger of energy-using systems and equipment, and inspecting the implementation of energy-saving design standards;
(2) verifying the measurement records of energy consumption such as electricity, gas, coal, oil, and municipal heat, as well as financial bills, and assessing the total energy consumption by category and item, per capita energy consumption, and energy consumption per unit building area;
(3) inspecting the operation status of energy-using systems and equipment, and reviewing the implementation of energy-saving management systems;
(4) inspecting the implementation of the recommendations for rational use of energy from the previous energy audit;
(5) identifying energy-using links or parts with energy-saving potential, and making recommendations for rational use of energy;
(6) reviewing the implementation of annual energy-saving plans and energy consumption quotas, and verifying the explanations of public institutions for using energy in excess of the energy consumption quotas;
(7) reviewing the operation of energy metering devices, and inspecting the authenticity and accuracy of energy consumption statistical data.

Article 35 The departments in charge of government office affairs of the State Council and of the local people's governments at or above the county level shall, jointly with the relevant departments, strengthen supervision and inspection of energy conservation of public institutions at the same level. The contents of supervision and inspection include:
(1) the formulation and implementation of annual energy conservation targets and implementation plans;
(2) the measurement, monitoring and statistics of energy consumption;
(3) the implementation of energy consumption quotas;
(4) the establishment of energy conservation management rules and regulations;
(5) the establishment of energy management posts and the implementation of the responsibility system for energy management posts;
(6) the energy-saving operation of energy-consuming systems and equipment;
(7) the conduct of energy audits;
(8) the allocation and use of official vehicles.
For public institutions with inadequate energy conservation rules and regulations or serious overuse of energy beyond the energy consumption quota, key supervision and inspection shall be conducted.

Article 37 Where a public institution commits any of the following acts, the department in charge of government office affairs of the people's government at the same level shall, jointly with the relevant departments, order it to make corrections within a time limit; if it fails to make corrections within the time limit, it shall be circulated as a notice, and the relevant organ shall impose sanctions on the person in charge of the public institution according to law:
(1) failing to formulate annual energy conservation targets and implementation plans, or failing to file the annual energy conservation targets and implementation plans as required;
(2) failing to implement the energy consumption measurement system, or failing to carry out household, category, and itemized measurement of energy consumption by type of energy use and energy-consuming system, and failing to conduct real-time monitoring of the energy consumption status;
(3) failing to designate a person responsible for energy consumption statistics, or failing to truthfully record the original data of energy consumption measurement and establish a statistical ledger;
(4) failing to submit a report on the energy consumption status of the previous year as required;
(5) using energy in excess of the energy consumption quota without giving an explanation to the department in charge of government office affairs of the people's government at the same level;
(6) failing to set up an energy management post, or failing to equip the key energy-consuming systems and equipment operation posts with professional technical personnel;
(7) failing to conduct energy audits as required, or failing to take measures to improve energy utilization efficiency based on the audit results;
(8) refusing or obstructing energy conservation supervision and inspection.
